From: Gaetan Lepage <gaetan@glepage.com>
Subject: [PATCH] hydra-queue-runner: skip builds with an unparseable drvPath

`get_not_finished_builds` parsed every row's drvPath into a `StorePath` and
collected the batch into a single `Result`, so one unparseable row failed the
whole query. The queue monitor then read no builds at all and nothing was ever
scheduled -- the only symptom being an ERROR line every retry interval, with
the machines sitting idle and the web interface showing a full queue.

Old deployments do carry such rows: builds created before the Rust queue runner
whose drvPath was recorded without the store directory are accepted by the Perl
and C++ code, which treat the column as an opaque string.

Skip those rows with a warning naming the build, so a handful of bad records
degrades to a handful of unscheduled builds instead of a dead queue.
---
--- a/subprojects/crates/db/src/connection.rs
+++ b/subprojects/crates/db/src/connection.rs
@@ -84,9 +84,26 @@
         )
         .fetch_all(&mut *self.conn)
         .await?;
-        rows.into_iter()
-            .map(|r| Ok(r.parse_paths(store_dir)?))
-            .collect()
+        // Skip rows we cannot make sense of rather than failing the batch.
+        // Very old deployments carry builds whose drvPath was recorded
+        // without the store directory, and returning `Err` here loses the
+        // whole queue -- the monitor reads no builds at all, so nothing is
+        // ever scheduled, with only a log line to say why.
+        Ok(rows
+            .into_iter()
+            .filter_map(|r| {
+                let (id, drvpath) = (r.id, r.drvpath.clone());
+                match r.parse_paths(store_dir) {
+                    Ok(build) => Some(build),
+                    Err(e) => {
+                        tracing::warn!(
+                            "skipping build {id}: cannot parse drvPath {drvpath:?}: {e}"
+                        );
+                        None
+                    }
+                }
+            })
+            .collect())
     }
 
     #[tracing::instrument(skip(self), err)]
